    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="lead"><a href="https://news.crunchbase.com/health-wellness-biotech/fitness-startup-funding-rebounding-ai-data-h1-2026/" {...ext}>Fitness and wellness startups</a> raised more than $3.6B in the first half of 2026, and almost every fitness company is doing something with AI. Some are building entire solutions around it. Others are turning a traditional product into an AI-enabled or AI-integrated one.</p>

      <p>I went through market reports, funding data, and company announcements, and I lay out what I found in this article: the first signals of what matters and what’s being built with it.</p>

      <nav class="contents" aria-label="Article contents">
        <p>In this article</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#four-jobs">The four jobs AI does for fitness brands</a></li>
          <li><a href="#venture-funding">Venture: Who’s getting funded in fitness, and why</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ai-coaching">AI coaching apps and personal trainers don’t sell the same thing</a></li>
          <li><a href="#gyms">Gyms meet AI inside their software</a></li>
          <li><a href="#data-fight">The data fight</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-fitness-ai-is-going">Where fitness AI is going</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-this-means">What this means for trainers and gyms</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <section id="four-jobs">
        <h2>The four jobs AI does for fitness brands</h2>

        <p>AI in the fitness industry does four jobs:</p>

        <ol class="jobs">
          <li><strong>Collects</strong> data</li>
          <li><strong>Predicts</strong> business outcomes from large datasets</li>
          <li><strong>Automates</strong> tasks</li>
          <li><strong>Generates</strong> a plan or message</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Outside fitness, AI drives two outcomes for business: it takes repeatable work off people’s hands, and it finds patterns in more data in a few minutes, when it would take days for a human to do so.</p>

        <p>Collecting data and predicting outcomes find the patterns. Automating tasks and generating plans do the repeatable work. Most brands use both.</p>

        <p>So the useful question is not “is it AI?” It is “which job does it do, and does that job make me money or save me time?”</p>

        <h3 id="established-vs-ai-first">Established platforms vs AI-first companies</h3>

        <ol class="split-list">
          <li>
            <p><strong>Established platforms integrate AI capabilities into what they already sell.</strong></p>
            <p>In gym software, for example, <a href="https://abcfitness.com/press-release/abc-fitness-ai-infrastructure/" {...ext}>ABC Fitness</a> added churn prediction, AI agents, and automated billing, and <a href="https://athletechnews.com/mindbody-ai-features/" {...ext}>Mindbody</a> added Clients at Risk and Big Spenders. Consumer platforms are doing the same (see the <a href="#ai-coaching">coaching section below</a>).</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><strong>The newer wave of AI-first companies builds the product around AI from day one.</strong></p>
            <p><strong>Two examples:</strong> <a href="https://partner.zing.coach/" {...ext}>Zing Coach</a> sells AI coaching straight to gyms, and it has a partnership with Les Mills. <a href="https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/cora" {...ext}>Cora</a> provides personalized fitness coaching using health and wearable data, and adapts your plans over time.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <figure class="figure-medium">
