      <section id="venture-funding">
        <h2>Venture: Who’s getting funded in fitness, and why</h2>

        <p>Fitness needs a sensing layer, because the data doesn’t exist until something captures it.</p>

        <p>Meaning, we only know how we recovered, how hard our heart worked on a run, because something on our wrist, finger, or chest recorded it.</p>

        <p>That’s why the biggest checks went to devices.</p>

        <p>The four device rounds <a href="https://news.crunchbase.com/health-wellness-biotech/fitness-startup-funding-rebounding-ai-data-h1-2026/" {...ext}>Crunchbase News</a> reported for the first half of 2026 (Whoop, Temple, Eight Sleep and Ultrahuman) raised about $723M. <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/31/whoop-valuation-10b-series-g-fundraise/" {...ext}>Whoop</a> took $575M of that in one round. Crunchbase named no AI coaching app rounds in the same period.</p>

        <figure class="figure-compact">
          <img
            src={images.funding.src}
            alt={images.funding.alt}
            width={images.funding.width}
            height={images.funding.height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>{images.funding.caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>Why? A device collects your data every day. An app only knows what you type in. Investors back whoever owns that data, because it makes AI coaching worth a monthly fee.</p>

        <p>The big platforms come second. <a href="https://athletechnews.com/fitness-wellness-startups-have-raised-3-6-billion-crunchbase/" {...ext}>Mindbody’s parent merged with EGYM</a> in a $7.5B deal. Garmin <a href="https://athletechnews.com/garmin-acquires-trainingpeaks-trainheroic/" {...ext}>bought TrainingPeaks</a>. They are buying AI coaching rather than waiting for startups to build it.</p>

        <figure class="figure-compact">
          <img
            src={images.wearableRounds.src}
            alt={images.wearableRounds.alt}
            width={images.wearableRounds.width}
            height={images.wearableRounds.height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>{images.wearableRounds.caption}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="ai-coaching">
        <h2>AI coaching apps and personal trainers don’t sell the same thing</h2>

        <p>AI fitness coaching comes from three places: funded startups like Zing Coach and Cora, small indie apps you can find in Product Hunt or other directories, and big apps adding AI to what they already had, like Strava and Runna, or Fitbit rebranding to <a href="https://www.androidauthority.com/google-health-app-coach-rebrand-3664205/" {...ext}>Google Health</a>.</p>

        <p>Every one of these apps runs on user data. Cora reads it from the wearable you already own. Google reads it from your Fitbit. That data shows what people actually do.</p>

        <p>Apps that are software only do the same four jobs:</p>

        <ol class="jobs">
          <li><strong>Collect:</strong> it tracks and logs each workout.</li>
          <li><strong>Generate:</strong> it writes the training plan.</li>
          <li><strong>Automate:</strong> it handles the delivery.</li>
          <li><strong>Predict:</strong> it reads how the week went and adjusts the plan.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>AI fitness coaching sells something narrower than a personal trainer does. It’s a way to get coaching advice and direction, and the data points you need to improve. That isn’t the same thing as personal training.</p>

        <p>The next stage for them is a better product, but still a niche one. The end goal is habit formation, and getting there would also change how coaching is delivered.</p>

        <p>The pressure on personal trainers is real, and it leads to one question: “<a href="/posts/ai-for-personal-trainers-top-uses-cases-tasks/">How can I use AI as a personal trainer?</a>” Many trainers haven’t started yet, and their clients aren’t pushing them. In a small <a href="https://www.issaonline.com/blog/post/the-human-advantage-how-ai-is-reshaping-not-replacing-personal" {...ext}>ISSA survey</a> of 90 students and graduates, 64% said their clients had not raised AI at all.</p>

        <p>Trainers who have started use AI to compete with the apps built to replace them.</p>

        <p><strong>Steer AI, and don’t let it steer you.</strong> In an AI coaching app, the data and the programming do the steering. The app reads the logs, changes the plan, and the client follows it.</p>

        <p>When you steer, AI does the logging, the summaries, and the first draft of a program. You read the client, decide what changes and why, and keep the relationship. The data informs your decision. It doesn’t make it.</p>

