        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="gyms">
        <h2>Gyms meet AI inside their software, and its job is automation</h2>

        <p>Most gyms run on “traditional” software, spreadsheets, or older systems. There’s no fitness-specific data yet, but a <a href="https://www.bluevine.com/blog/small-business-ai-trends-report-2026" {...ext}>Bluevine survey</a> of US owners of businesses with 2 to 249 employees shows how much small businesses spend on AI each month:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>33% spend nothing on AI tools</li>
          <li>28% spend $25 to $99 a month</li>
          <li>16% spend $100 to $249 a month</li>
          <li>10% spend $250 or more</li>
        </ul>

        <p>With budgets this small, most gyms will meet AI inside the software that already holds their member data: bookings, payments, check-ins, and inquiries.</p>

        <p>You have three options right now. You can build your own system and own everything. You can keep one central layer that holds your data, and connect tools to it. Or you can run several specialist tools, each holding its own slice.</p>

        <p>Whichever you pick, AI does four jobs inside a gym:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Collect:</strong> it pulls check-ins, bookings, payments, and inquiries into one member record, so nothing lives in five places.</li>
          <li><strong>Predict:</strong> it spots which members are about to leave, while you can still do something about it.</li>
          <li><strong>Automate:</strong> it handles the small repeat tasks, like payment reminders, booking confirmations, and first replies to inquiries.</li>
          <li><strong>Generate:</strong> it writes the training plans, follow-up messages, and weekly reports your staff would otherwise write by hand.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The first job decides the rest. AI can only predict, automate, and generate from what it has collected.</p>

        {/* Case study slot: FxM gym case studies go here once published. */}
      </section>

      <section id="data-fight">
        <h2>The data fight</h2>

        <p>Across AI, the winners so far are the companies that collect data and do something with it at a larger scale than everyone else. Fitness AI works the same way. That’s why there’s a data fight: some platforms are closing their data off, and others are opening it.</p>

        <p><a href="https://communityhub.strava.com/developers-api-7/api-agreement-update-how-data-appears-on-3rd-party-apps-7636" {...ext}>Strava</a> started restricting outside access in 2024. In 2026, <a href="https://the5krunner.com/2026/10/14/garmin-api-intervals-third-party-apps/" {...ext}>Garmin</a> paused new developer access and banned apps from passing its data on. <a href="https://developers.google.com/health/about?hl=en" {...ext}>Google</a> retired the old Fitbit connection the same year and now puts every app through a privacy and security review.</p>

        <p><a href="https://www.wodify.com/blog/wodify-mcp-connector-api-access" {...ext}>Wodify</a> went the other way. It lets gym owners ask questions about their own data in Claude or ChatGPT. Its reason: gym software usually gives owners fixed reports, and owners want to build their own dashboards and automations. In Wodify’s words, “your data is yours.”</p>

        <p>Customers are watching the same fight from the other side: who gets to see their data. In <a href="https://abcfitness.com/press-release/summer_wellness_watch_report/" {...ext}>ABC Fitness’s survey</a>, 55% of consumers said data and privacy worry them when they use AI fitness tools. Oura learned that in 2025. A <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/09/smart-ring-maker-ouras-ceo-addresses-recent-backlash-says-future-is-a-cloud-of-wearables/" {...ext}>public backlash</a> over who it works with went viral, and its CEO had to <a href="https://fortune.com/2025/09/09/oura-ceo-tom-hale-data-privacy-oura-ring-defense-department-palantir/" {...ext}>promise publicly</a> never to sell user data.</p>

        <p><strong>My take:</strong> owning your data has two parts: you control who gets in, and you keep the right to take it out. Strava and Garmin show the first part. They decide who reads their data, even though it has cost them goodwill with users and developers. A gym needs both parts.</p>

        <p>Toward outsiders, you decide who gets access. Toward your own software, you make sure you can get your data out.</p>

        <p>Your check-ins, attendance, and app logs are what will help you improve your product, deliver it better, and keep and grow your members. Keep them in a system you control and can export. If a larger network can teach your system more, use it, as long as your data stays yours to take with you.</p>

        <aside class="related-read wearable-cta">
          <p class="kicker">Related guide</p>
