          <p>See how trainers turn wearable data into programme decisions, a clear client deliverable and a service they can price sustainably.</p>
          <a class="button" href="/posts/wearable-data-and-data-driven-coaching/">Read the wearable data guide</a>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-fitness-ai-is-going">
        <h2>Where fitness AI is going</h2>

        <p>Three signals show where fitness AI is heading:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Human coaching becomes the premium:</strong> AI apps compete on price, while <a href="https://athletechnews.com/future-pulls-the-plug-on-ai-personal-training-commits-to-human-coaches/" {...ext}>Future</a> scrapped its free AI beta and sells human coaching from $129 a month.</li>
          <li><strong>Access to your own data:</strong> whoever holds the member data decides which tools read it and what AI learns from it.</li>
          <li><strong>Automation and prediction become the main job:</strong> of the four jobs, these two will pay off first for fitness businesses: predicting who’s about to leave, and automating the work that keeps them.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="what-this-means">
        <h2>What this means for trainers and gyms</h2>

        <p>AI in the fitness industry comes down to one question: who holds the data, and what do they do with it? The answer shows up at every level.</p>

        <ul class="takeaways">
          <li><strong>Investors pay for data they can’t get anywhere else.</strong> Devices raised about $723M in the first half of this year, while Crunchbase named no AI coaching app rounds. A device records the data itself. An app has to get it from somewhere else.</li>
          <li><strong>AI coaching apps sell a plan. Trainers sell judgment.</strong> The apps are a cheaper way to get advice, which isn’t the same as being coached. Trainers are fine for now, as long as they use AI for the logging and the first draft, and keep making the calls themselves.</li>
          <li><strong>For gyms, the data comes before the AI.</strong> AI can only work on what it can reach. So get your check-ins, bookings, and payments into one place you control and can export. Then start where it pays first: spotting who’s about to leave and automating the follow-up that keeps them.</li>
        </ul>

